@page "/components/charts"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Charts

<DocsHeader Title="Charts" Eyebrow="Data">
    <Lead>Line, area, bar, donut and sparkline charts as plain SVG, themed with the Stil tokens and readable by screen readers.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    The charts are in a separate package, <code>Stil.Charts</code>, so apps that do not draw charts stay small. They
    render on the server and need no JavaScript, so they also work in static server rendering. Hover effects and
    tooltips are CSS and native SVG titles.
</p>

<h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
<p>Reference <code>Stil.Charts</code> next to <code>Stil</code>, link its stylesheet after <code>&lt;StilHead /&gt;</code> and add the namespace to <code>_Imports.razor</code>:</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@Setup" />

<h2 id="line">Line charts</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Revenue)">
    <p>
        <code>Items</code> holds one item per category and <code>Category</code> names it. Each
        <code>StilChartSeries</code> reads one number from every item; it can be any number type, and nullable. A
        <code>null</code> leaves a gap. <code>Format</code> formats the values in the current culture. With two to four
        series, each line is named at its end when there is room; the legend always names them.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="area">Area charts</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Area)">
    <p>
        <code>Area</code> fills under the line, and <code>ShowMarkers</code> marks every point (by default only the last
        one is marked). <code>CategoryFormat</code> formats dates and numbers on the category axis. A line chart starts
        its axis at zero; set <code>BeginAtZero="false"</code> to zoom in on small changes. <code>Summary</code> tells
        screen readers what the chart shows in your words.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="bars">Bar charts</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Bars)">
    <p>
        Series are drawn side by side, or on top of each other with <code>Stacked</code>; stacked bars add a total to the
        tooltips and the data table. <code>ShowValues</code> writes the value (or the stack's total) at the end of each bar.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="horizontal">Horizontal bars</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(HorizontalBars)">
    <p>
        <code>Orientation="Horizontal"</code> suits long category names and ranked lists. A series can have its own
        <code>Color</code>, any CSS color or token.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="donut">Donut charts</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Donut)">
    <p>
        <code>StilDonutChart</code> shows the parts of a whole from the first series, with the total in the middle and a
        legend that lists each part's value and share. Parts beyond <code>MaxSlices</code> (6, the number of chart colors)
        are combined into "Other". Use it for a few parts; for many, or to compare sizes, use a bar chart.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="sparklines">Sparklines</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Sparklines)">
    <p>
        <code>StilSparkline</code> is a small line without axes that fills its container's width, for example in a
        <a href="components/stat-cards">stat card</a>. With a <code>Label</code>, screen readers hear it with the first,
        last, lowest and highest value. Without one it is decorative and hidden, so only leave it out when the numbers
        are already in the text.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(DataTables)">
    <p>
        Every chart is a <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> whose drawing is an <code>&lt;svg role="img"&gt;</code>, named by the
        <code>Title</code> and described by a generated summary of the data. <code>DataTable</code> offers the numbers as
        a real table: a "Show data" toggle (the default), a table only screen readers find (<code>Hidden</code>), or
        <code>None</code> when the same numbers are elsewhere on the page. Series are told apart by the legend, labels and
        table, not by color alone, and in Windows high-contrast mode the text and axes use system colors. The texts
        (<code>ShowDataText</code>, <code>EmptyText</code>, <code>CategoryText</code>, <code>MissingText</code>, …) can be
        translated.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="theming">Theming</h2>
<p>
    The series colors are the tokens <code>--stil-chart-1</code> to <code>--stil-chart-6</code>, a palette with separate
    light and dark steps, used in the order the series are declared. Override them like any <code>--stil-*</code> token,
    on <code>:root</code> or on a wrapper. <code>--stil-chart-surface</code> is the color of the gaps between touching
    marks; set it to the page background when a chart is not on a card.
</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@Tokens" Language="css" />

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilLineChart<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilBarChart<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDonutChart<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilChartSeries<,>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilSparkline)" />

@code {
    private const string Setup = """
        @* Components/App.razor *@
        <StilHead />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Stil.Charts/stil-charts.css"]" />

        @* _Imports.razor *@
        @using Stil.Charts
        """;

    private const string Tokens = """
        :root {
            --stil-chart-1: #6741d9;
            --stil-chart-2: light-dark(#e8590c, #ff922b);
            --stil-chart-surface: var(--stil-color-body-bg);
        }
        """;
}
